{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Research Translation for Learning Design","title":"Small-N Testing and Design Claims","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting small-n testing and design claims to moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-01","statement":"Explain why small-N moderated testing is strong for surfacing usability problems but weak for outcome claims"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-02","statement":"Convert an overreaching efficacy claim into a scoped problem-and-revision claim the sample can support"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-03","statement":"Use observation frequency (e.g., 4 of 5) to prioritize revisions and to flag low-frequency signals for watching rather than acting"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-04","statement":"Reject outcome language (\"improves learning\") when no comparison or baseline exists"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_small_n_testing_and_design_claims","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"04","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/07/03"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_artifact_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Small-N Testing and Design Claims\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Small N Testing And Design Claims, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/07-04-small-n-testing-and-design-claims.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Small-N Testing and Design Claims: Small N Testing And Design Claims, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA five-person usability test is one of the most useful tools a learning designer has, and one of the most frequently over-interpreted. The classic finding from Nielsen and Landauer is that a handful of testers, often around five, surface the large majority of *usability problems* in an interface, because problems are encountered repeatedly across users. But that same handful supports almost nothing about *learning outcomes*, because outcome claims require a baseline, a comparison group, and enough participants to distinguish signal from noise. The skill this section builds is matching the claim to what the method can bear: small-N moderated testing licenses problem-finding and revision, not efficacy.\n\nThis matters because the most common failure in a design report is a claim the method cannot carry: \"our test proves the lesson improves learning.\" The sentence overreaches by a wide margin, and a reviewer will see it immediately. Learning to scope claims to the evidence is what separates a credible design study from a hopeful one.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Explain why small-N moderated testing is strong for surfacing usability problems but weak for outcome claims.\n- Convert an overreaching efficacy claim into a scoped problem-and-revision claim the sample can support.\n- Use observation frequency (e.g., 4 of 5) to prioritize revisions and to flag low-frequency signals for watching rather than acting.\n- Reject outcome language (\"improves learning\") when no comparison or baseline exists.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nAfter five moderated sessions, a teammate drafts the headline: \"Our usability test proves the redesigned lesson improves learning, since testers did better and liked it.\" Two things have gone wrong at once. First, \"did better\" has no baseline; there is nothing the testers were compared against. Second, even with a baseline, five participants and no control condition cannot support a population-level efficacy claim. What the five sessions *can* support is concrete and valuable: which interface moments caused friction, how often, and what revision each one warrants. The task is to throw away the efficacy sentence and keep the problem-and-revision findings.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nSmall-N moderated testing is a formative method. Its logic and its limits both follow from how usability problems are distributed:\n\n- **Why few users find most problems:** a serious usability problem tends to trip many users, so its probability of appearing in a sample rises quickly with each added tester. A handful of users therefore catches most *high-frequency* problems. This is a claim about problem detection, not about effect sizes.\n\nThe Nielsen-Landauer model makes this concrete: the expected fraction of a problem set found with `n` testers is \\$1 - (1-p)^n\\$, where `p` is the probability one tester hits a given problem. Move the sliders and watch why five testers can surface most high-frequency problems yet why no number of testers turns problem-finding into an efficacy claim.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Problem discovery vs. number of testers\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"p\",\"label\":\"Probability one tester hits a given problem\",\"min\":0.1,\"max\":0.6,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0.31},{\"name\":\"n\",\"label\":\"Number of testers\",\"min\":1,\"max\":15,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"testers\",\"default\":5}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Fraction of problems found\",\"unit\":\"\",\"expr\":\"1 - (1-p)^n\"},{\"label\":\"Fraction still undetected\",\"unit\":\"\",\"expr\":\"(1-p)^n\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"n\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nNotice the curve flattens: with p near 0.31, five testers already find roughly 85 percent of problems, but doubling to ten testers adds only a sliver. The model rewards small-N problem-finding and says nothing about whether the redesign improves learning.\n- **Why few users cannot prove outcomes:** an efficacy claim (\"the redesign improves learning\") is a comparative, population-level statement. It needs a baseline or control, random assignment or matching, and a sample large enough to separate a real effect from noise. None of these is present in a five-person walkthrough.\n- **What the method licenses:** scoped claims of the form \"behavior B occurred in k of n sessions, so we revised feature F and will recheck.\" Frequency drives priority; a 4-of-5 problem is acted on now, a 1-of-5 signal is logged and watched, not yet treated as real.\n\nThe course anchors guide interpretation. **Cognitive load theory** helps you read *why* a friction point occurred: a learner who clicks Back instead of Next may be managing load through a mislabeled control, which points to a specific revision. **Mayer's principles** help you distinguish a usability problem (the control is hard to find) from a learning-design problem (the content itself overloads), which warrant different fixes. **UDL learner variability** warns that five testers may all share a background that hides a problem affecting other learners, so absence of a problem in five sessions is not evidence the problem does not exist.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nTrace one finding from an overreaching claim to a claim a five-person test can carry.\n\n**Before (claim drafted after five moderated sessions):**\n\n> Our usability test proves the redesigned lesson improves learning, since testers did better and liked it.\n\n1. Five sessions with no control cannot support an efficacy or population claim.\n2. \"Did better\" names no baseline and no measure.\n3. \"Liked it\" is a satisfaction reaction, not evidence of learning.\n\n**After (observation-to-revision trace entry):**\n\n| Observation | Frequency | Revision | Recheck |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Clicked Back instead of Next at frame 2 | 4 of 5 | Relabel button to \"Continue\" and move it right | Watch frame-2 path next round |\n| Hovered glossary popup with no use | 1 of 5 | None yet; low-frequency signal | Flag to watch |\n\nThe revised entry scopes each claim to what five sessions bear: a named behavior, its frequency, the revision it triggers, and a recheck. The 4-of-5 navigation problem is acted on immediately; the 1-of-5 glossary hover is logged, not treated as a finding.\n\nOne rejected line: a statement that the lesson \"improves learning.\" I declined it because no outcome comparison was run and the sample cannot support a population claim; keeping it would invite a reviewer to discount the whole report.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Scope each observation by its frequency\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"4 of 5 testers clicked Back instead of Next at frame 2. How should this observation be treated?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Act now: relabel the control and recheck next round\",\"Log and watch; too few sessions to act\",\"Report as proof the lesson improves learning\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"A 4-of-5 problem is high-frequency and actionable. Relabel the button to \\\"Continue,\\\" move it right, and watch the frame-2 path next round. Frequency drives priority.\"},{\"prompt\":\"1 of 5 testers hovered the glossary popup without using it. How should THIS be treated?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Act now with a redesign\",\"Log it as a low-frequency signal to watch, not yet a finding\",\"Conclude the glossary is broken\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A 1-of-5 signal is logged and watched, not treated as real yet. Acting on every single-occurrence behavior would waste the revision budget and over-fit to one tester.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The scoped report keeps observation, frequency, revision, and recheck for each finding, and contains no outcome claim, because five sessions with no control cannot license one. Frequency is the unit of trust.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore writing any claim from a small-N test, check that the claim type matches the method:\n\n1. **Claim-type check:** Is this a problem-finding claim (supported) or an outcome claim (unsupported without a control and adequate N)? Rewrite any outcome claim as a problem-and-revision claim.\n2. **Frequency check:** Does the claim report how many of n sessions showed the behavior, so a reader can weigh it? Treat high-frequency observations as actionable and low-frequency ones as watch items.\n3. **Baseline check:** If the claim implies improvement, is there an explicit comparison? If not, remove the comparative language.\n\nA claim that fails the type check is not merely imprecise; it misrepresents the strength of the evidence and undermines the credible findings around it.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Five testers all liked the redesigned lesson and finished faster than the old one was 'supposed' to take. A teammate writes 'the redesign improves learning.' What is the core problem?\" explanation=\"Liking is a reaction, not an outcome, and there is no baseline or control group, so five participants cannot support a population-level efficacy claim. The honest claim is about problems found and revisions warranted.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Nothing; satisfaction plus speed proves learning.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"There is no baseline or control, and satisfaction is not a learning outcome.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The sample is fine; the wording just needs polish.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why the same five-person test that reliably surfaces most usability PROBLEMS cannot support a claim that the redesign IMPROVES LEARNING. Reference both the problem-discovery model and the requirements of an efficacy claim.\" answer=\"Problems are detected because a serious problem trips many users, so its chance of appearing rises fast with each tester (1 minus (1-p)^n approaches 1 quickly); five testers catch most high-frequency problems. An efficacy claim is a comparative, population-level statement that requires a baseline or control, random assignment or matching, and a sample large enough to separate signal from noise. A five-person walkthrough has none of these, so it licenses problem-and-revision claims (observation, frequency, revision, recheck) but not outcome claims.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nOne misreading inflates satisfaction into learning: testers \"liked it,\" so the lesson must work. Liking is a reaction, not an outcome, and the two often diverge. A second misreading treats absence of a problem in five sessions as proof the problem is gone; five testers who share a background can collectively miss a problem that affects a different group entirely. Small-N testing tells you about the problems your testers hit, with the frequencies you observed, and is silent about both untested learners and untested outcomes.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the method to a quiz-feedback prototype. After five moderated sessions, the raw notes say learners \"seemed confused by the feedback.\" Walk it into scoped claims. First, identify behaviors, not impressions: 3 of 5 learners reread the feedback box twice before answering the next item, and 2 of 5 clicked \"Submit\" again expecting new feedback. The 3-of-5 rereading is a high-frequency friction point, so the revision is concrete: shorten the feedback to one actionable sentence and recheck rereading next round. The 2-of-5 double-submit is mid-frequency, so disable the button post-submit and watch. Crucially, the report does *not* claim the feedback \"improves performance,\" because no baseline quiz was run. The walkthrough turns a vague impression into prioritized revisions whose strength a reader can judge from the frequencies.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nFor every sentence in a small-N report, ask: what is the largest claim these five sessions could honestly license? A strong reader instantly flags any comparative or population word (\"improves,\" \"proves,\" \"more effective\") that lacks a baseline, and rewrites it as an observation-frequency-revision triple. The habit is to read the method section first and let it cap the claims, rather than reading the claims and hoping the method supports them. Frequencies become the unit of trust: a claim without a k-of-n is a claim you cannot yet weigh.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe usability script and trace are strong when every finding is stated as observation, frequency, revision, and recheck, and when the report contains no outcome claim unbacked by a comparison. The README should note at least one observation you deliberately did *not* act on because its frequency was too low, demonstrating that you let frequency drive priority. Visual polish of the report is the last layer; the load-bearing layer is the claim-to-method match.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nCarry the scoping discipline into a high-stakes context where a stakeholder wants an efficacy headline you cannot honestly provide. The transfer is to hold the claim line: report the prioritized usability fixes the five sessions support, and explicitly state what a *summative* efficacy study would require (a control group, random assignment, adequate N, a pre-registered outcome). Naming the gap is more credible than papering over it, and it converts pressure for an unsupported claim into a concrete proposal for the study that could actually earn it. The skill transfers because it is about matching claim strength to evidence strength under any amount of stakeholder enthusiasm.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design\", \"ail606_supplement_small_n_testing_and_design_claims\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/07-04-small-n-testing-and-design-claims-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"07.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace for AIL 606 07.04. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_small_n_testing_and_design_claims\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Small-N Testing and Design Claims, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_small_n_testing_and_design_claims","stem":"You ran a moderated usability test with five learners. Which claim does that small-N test actually license?","options":["The lesson significantly improves learning outcomes for the population.","Most learners hit the same navigation snag at the branch screen, so the design has a usability problem worth fixing.","The new design is more effective than the old one, with statistical confidence.","Learners in general prefer this lesson."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Small-N moderated testing surfaces usability problems and design failures, not population-level efficacy or significance claims. The supported claim is about observed friction, not generalized effect.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_smalln_proves_efficacy","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_small_n_testing_and_design_claims","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-01","ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_small_n_testing_and_design_claims","stem":"Which entry belongs in an observation-to-revision trace rather than in a results section?","options":["Mean score was 72 percent.","Observation: 4 of 5 learners clicked Back instead of Next at frame 2. Revision: relabel the button and move it; recheck at next session.","The lesson was well received.","Participants were recruited by email."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An observation-to-revision trace pairs a concrete observed behavior with the specific design change it triggered and a recheck plan. Means and recruitment notes are not that link.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_observation_without_action","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_small_n_testing_and_design_claims","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You use an AI tool on your five session transcripts. Which use respects what small-N evidence can bear?","options":["Ask the AI to compute a p-value and declare the design effective.","Ask the AI to cluster recurring friction moments across the five sessions, then you decide which clusters justify a design change.","Have the AI invent two more participants so the sample looks larger.","Use the AI to write a conclusion that the lesson works for all learners."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The AI can help organize observed friction patterns; it must not manufacture sample size or convert usability findings into efficacy claims. Judgment about which patterns matter stays yours.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_smalln_proves_efficacy","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_small_n_testing_and_design_claims","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_small_n_testing_and_design_claims","stem":"Only one of your five testers struggled with the glossary popup. How should the trace handle a one-of-five observation?","options":["Treat it as a confirmed design defect and overhaul the glossary.","Log it as a low-frequency signal, note it may be idiosyncratic, and flag it to watch in the next round before committing a redesign.","Ignore it entirely because it was only one person.","Report it as affecting 20 percent of all learners."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A single occurrence is weak evidence in small-N testing. The disciplined move is to record it, mark uncertainty, and watch for recurrence rather than over- or under-reacting.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_one_voice_overgeneralized","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_small_n_testing_and_design_claims","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-04-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_smalln_proves_efficacy","pattern":"smalln_treated_as_efficacy_trial","description":"Drawing population-level effectiveness or statistical-significance claims from a handful of moderated usability sessions","trigger":"learner reports that a five-person test shows the design improves learning or beats the old version with confidence","feedback":"Small-N moderated testing supports usability and design-failure claims, not efficacy. Scope the claim to observed friction in this sample.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_observation_without_action","pattern":"observation_logged_without_revision","description":"Recording what testers did but never linking each observation to a specific design change and recheck","trigger":"learner lists behaviors or scores but the trace shows no observation-to-revision pairing","feedback":"Pair each meaningful observation with the design change it triggered and a plan to recheck it. The trace is the link, not the list.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_4_small_n_testing_and_design_claims_one_voice_overgeneralized","pattern":"single_observation_overgeneralized","description":"Treating a one-of-five occurrence as either a confirmed defect or a percentage of the whole population","trigger":"learner converts a single tester's struggle into a population rate or an immediate redesign","feedback":"Record low-frequency signals as uncertain and watch for recurrence before committing a redesign or reporting a rate.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"c872fe32b614cd325cf63667bbd0d00a7adbf9f1d8a8f336292f69e032b8d1d8","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}